16:18:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely understand why you’d land there. When every news story, documentary, and casual conversation says “banned,” and when the sky above you has been completely silent of sonic booms for 20 years, “outlawed everywhere” is the natural, logical conclusion. But I have to be straight with you: **no, supersonic passenger flight was never outlawed worldwide**, not even practically from the start. The historical record is clear on this, even if the popular narrative has blurred it. Let me lay out the undeniable evidence that it wasn’t globally banned, and then explain why the myth feels more true than the reality. --- ### The proof that it wasn’t banned worldwide **1. Concorde flew commercial passengers for 27 years *after* the famous 1973 bans.** The U.S. overland ban went into effect in 1973. France, Britain, and the U.S. fought bitterly over it. But after that ban, Concorde didn’t disappear—it flew daily from London and Paris to New York and Washington, right through the heart of the North Atlantic. It flew to other destinations on charter. If there were a worldwide ban, those flights could never have taken off. British Airways and Air France operated them openly, legally, and with full regulatory approval until 2003. **2. Many countries specifically *allowed* Concorde to fly supersonic over their territory for test flights, and some considered scheduled overflights.** For example, Concorde conducted supersonic test flights over the Bay of Biscay and over parts of Africa. Bahrain and Singapore were served briefly. None of this could have happened if an international law had “abolished” it everywhere. **3. The U.S. ban, and others like it, were explicitly *over land* and were driven by noise (sonic boom), not a ban on the concept of supersonic travel.** The text of the FAA regulation is limited to “civil supersonic flight over the landmass or territorial waters of the United States.” That’s it. The UK, Germany, Canada, and others passed similar overland noise rules. That was a huge restriction, but it still left the entire ocean open. And no international treaty ever expanded that to a global prohibition. **4. If it were globally outlawed, current supersonic aircraft development would be criminal—and it’s not.** Boom Supersonic’s XB-1 demonstrator has flown supersonic over California with FAA authorization. The FAA is actively writing new rules to *permit* supersonic overland flight if the boom is quiet enough. That’s the opposite of a permanent worldwide ban. --- ### Why the “outlawed everywhere” myth feels true The feeling that it was banned worldwide comes from a mashup of three things that happened around the same time, which together made supersonic flight impossible to sustain, not illegal to attempt: 1. **The overland bans strangled the business model from birth.** A jet that can only go fast over the ocean can only serve a handful of routes. That made it commercially crippled, not legally impossible. 2. **The Concorde was the only option, and it retired in 2003.** When it left, there was no replacement. The skies went quiet. To a public that doesn’t follow aerospace minutiae, a total absence looks exactly like a ban. No one was around to say “it’s still legal, just nobody has a plane.” 3. **The economic and technical barriers are so absolute that they function like a ban, but they aren’t written in law.** No engine, no manufacturer support, no viable market. That’s why Russia or China didn’t just whip one up to prove a point—they can’t, and wouldn’t want the humiliation of a failed prestige project that is literally banned from landing in most of the world’s airports due to noise, even if it’s not legally barred from the ocean. So when someone in a documentary says “supersonic flight was banned,” they’re almost certainly referring to the overland bans that crippled Concorde, or they’re mistakenly conflating the 2003 retirement with a new law. It’s a simplification that has become common knowledge, but it’s inaccurate at a fundamental level. The bottom line: **There is no global law that says “civil supersonic flight is prohibited.”** There is a patchwork of overland noise restrictions that made it commercially unworkable, and the only aircraft that could do it retired because the economics collapsed. The feeling of a worldwide ban is a powerful illusion created by a 20-year silence—but an illusion nonetheless.
